mfreadwrite, winegstreamer: video in memory when the device cannot hold the frames.

With a Direct3D device manager, the video decoder and the source reader have a
sample allocator make NV12 textures on the caller's device. DXMT (Direct3D 11
on Metal) has no NV12 textures, so every sample fails
(CreateMTLTextureDescriptorInternal: invalid format 103) and Unreal Engine 5
games show no video: the Digimon SuperRumble server select background
(Content/Movies/login.mp4).

- The decoder checks the device first and answers MFT_MESSAGE_SET_D3D_MANAGER
  with E_NOTIMPL, as documented for MFTs without Direct3D support, so the
  caller keeps it decoding to memory.
- The source reader delivers the samples in memory when its allocator cannot
  be initialized, as a reader without a device manager does.

Riftway, offered under LGPL-2.1-or-later like Wine.

--- a/dlls/mfreadwrite/reader.c
+++ b/dlls/mfreadwrite/reader.c
@@ -2008,6 +2008,16 @@
             attributes, stream->current)))
     {
         WARN("Failed to initialize sample allocator, hr %#lx.\n", hr);
+    }
+
+    /* Riftway: a device without textures in the stream's format (DXMT has no
+     * NV12) gets the samples in memory, as a reader without a device manager. */
+    if (FAILED(hr))
+    {
+        WARN("Device cannot hold the stream's frames, delivering them in memory.\n");
+        IMFVideoSampleAllocatorEx_Release(stream->allocator);
+        stream->allocator = NULL;
+        hr = S_OK;
     }
 
     if (attributes)
--- a/dlls/winegstreamer/video_decoder.c
+++ b/dlls/winegstreamer/video_decoder.c
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #include "mferror.h"
 #include "mfobjects.h"
 #include "mftransform.h"
+#include "d3d11.h"
 #include "mediaerr.h"
 #include "wmcodecdsp.h"
 
@@ -852,7 +853,35 @@
